FORMAT of a brief (house style — read .agi/nodes/hypothesis/l3-dispatch-dry-run.md and .agi/nodes/hypothesis/l3w3-advisor-brief.md first and mirror their body shape): sections CLAIM, WHY (cite the owner verbatim lines it comes from, quoted short), FILES (one per line as "path :: symbol or section", every path verified to exist with ls/grep; new files marked NEW), DESIGN (concrete: field names, row shapes, command flags, defaults), TESTS (red-first, named), GATE (what a parent must see to accept proved), NOT IN SCOPE (what neighbouring briefs own), SOURCE (owner text section + which numbered owner quote). Max 450 words of body. Never invent an owner decision: anything not in the owner verbatim text is labelled "(director proposal)". The testable_claim is ONE falsifiable sentence.
CONSTRAINTS: the body must contain NO "&&" anywhere (the minting tool splits on it — write "and"). Plain markdown, no frontmatter, no title line (the tool adds it). Do not write the file inside the repo. Save the body to ${SCRATCH}/<slug>.md and return {slug, parent, title, testable_claim, body_path, files}.`
const DEFAULT_BRIEFS = [
  { slug: 'l3w4-seat-registry', parent: 'goal:g17', scope: 'The seat registry: extend the ladder roles table (or a config node it references — decide and justify from [ladder].md/[config].md) into one row per SEAT: seat name, role, ladder tier, harness, model, effort, settings, session kind (remote-control | tty | fire-and-forget), personality ref (mantle/context node), handoff file, current session id + transcript pin, rotated-by (prime | quorum | advisor), owning goal. dispatch.py --seat <name> resolves a spawn from the row; rotate.py reads the pin from it. Include the owner model-table change as data (owner quote (4) overrides (3)): prime = Fable 5.1 max ultracode; three advisors (the quorum) = Opus 5 MAX; every perpetual-goal director-kid = Opus 5 HIGH; liaison = Sonnet 5 high; pi parents and kids unchanged. Owner quotes (2), (3), (4).' },
  { slug: 'l3w4-seat-transport', parent: 'goal:g17', scope: 'Perpetual seats run as interactive TTY sessions in tmux (claude --remote-control where the seat row says remote-control; plain claude with a TTY for tty), never claude -p (one bounded turn, unaddressable). send.py send --to <seat> writes the inbox AND types a one-line nudge into the seat tmux window (tmux send-keys), so a live seat wakes; ephemeral parents/kids stay fire-and-forget. Reuse rotate.py spawn transport (it already launches remote-control windows) — say exactly which function generalises. Owner quotes (1) and (2) "all have remote sessions just in case", gloss "Transport change required".' },
  { slug: 'l3w4-liaison-seat', parent: 'goal:g17', scope: 'The owner-liaison director-kid: Sonnet 5, effort high, remote-control session, its own seat row under goal:g17, rotated by the quorum, becomes the owner primary contact (owner ↔ liaison ↔ quorum); banks owner decisions into the graph (the way HANDOFF §6 does today) and relays quorum questions. First slice of wave 4: it can start as a remote-control Sonnet-high session the prime spawns with a short brief (brief.py tier=liaison) before the loops exist. Owner quotes (2) and (3).' },
  { slug: 'l3w4-seat-rotation-loops', parent: 'goal:g17', scope: 'Perpetual rotation for every non-prime seat at director_rotate_at 0.35: the prime holds an alarm on each advisor seat pinned meter and talks to it when it is time (seat writes its handoff, successor spawned from the seat row, confirms, predecessor exits); advisors rotate the directors; the quorum rotates the liaison; the advisors keep parts of the prime handoff current and rotate him together with him. No Roman numerals below the prime — seats keep plain names (adv-alive, dir-g15, liaison), generation stamped in the seat handoff. Each layer lasts longer than the one below. Owner quote (1), gloss "Seat lifecycle".' },
  { slug: 'l3w4-quorum-reviews', parent: 'goal:g12.3', scope: 'Review moves down to the quorum: the three advisors review each round and judge outcomes through their visions, each posting aligned | adjust in the room; 3-0 or 2-1 stands and the prime only commits; 1-1-1 or a morals flag triggers send.py audience prime. The prime is inbox-only, never a room member; an audience is bounded and when the quorum finishes speaking Belam exits the room and receives no further group traffic. Concrete: a vote record shape (where it lives — room post fields or a node), how season.py judge consumes it, what send.py needs (audience close, prime exit). Owner trigger quote + quote (2) "once quorum finishes speaking to Belam, he exits the room" + quote (4) comms rule: collapsed ladder gives director-kids no free comms to Belam; the expanded hierarchy allows free director-kid lateral comms and limited director-kid vertical comms to other director-kids; no director reaches Belam except through the quorum, which IS Belam to anyone else — design the send.py audience/room permissions from this.' },
  { slug: 'l3w4-context-doc-nodes', parent: 'goal:g13.1', scope: 'Design docs under .agi/context/*.md have no node, so no grid version, no edited_by, no write_guard — Belam II appended owner text to l3-command-ladder-brief.md and the guard stayed silent. Mint doc nodes (type doc exists — check [doc].md) with payload_ref for every .agi/context/*.md design doc (l3-command-ladder-brief.md, season-ladder-and-morals-brief.md, goals-preamble.md; NOT INJECTION.md, which is derived), make write_guard.py warn on any write under .agi/context/ that is not a logged payload write, and route future owner-text sections through write.py payload. Source: Belam II DM addendum relaying the owner (06:55 UTC): "mint doc nodes with payload_ref for every .agi/context/*.md design doc, write_guard warns on any write under .agi/context/ that is not a logged payload write".' },
  { slug: 'l3w4-drafter-seat', parent: 'goal:g17', scope: 'The DRAFTER seat (owner quote (5), verbatim in the owner section): a Sonnet 5 (or pi ~z-ai/glm-flash-latest) seat that summons the brief-drafting workflow and runs it; anyone needing a brief drafted goes through the drafter (send.py send --to drafter with the owner-text pointer + slugs), never drafts by hand. Requirements from the owner text: drafting agents are Sonnet at the top effort; the drafting is agent-agnostic — the drafter is spawned through dispatch.py from its seat row so model/harness can be flipped (claude-code Sonnet runs the Workflow script; a pi GLM drafter drafts through its own kids); and the owner must still see the interactive update — so the drafter seat runs as a remote-control session (see l3w4-seat-transport) whose URL the owner can watch, and the workflow script becomes a build node payload under g17 (e.g. extensions/agi/workflows/draft-briefs.js — NEW, decide the path from the repo layout) so any seat can summon it; the current script lives in a Claude session dir and is not graph content. Concrete: the seat row, the request format, where drafts land (scratch then write.py create + note under the named parents), the acceptance gate (critic pass + the requester reviews).' },
  { slug: 'l3w4-telemetry-seat-status', parent: 'goal:g16', scope: 'Telemetry renders live stats in the graph including seat status: build nodes under goal:g16 whose payload renders, from the seat registry + spawn_budget + rotate.py meter pins + telemetry_rollup, a live view (viewport.py --live already draws agents as spiders — extend it or add a seats frame) so "the graph literally has everything". Owner quote (3) last two sentences. Keep it a thin slice: what is measured, where it renders, one test.' },
]
const BRIEFS = (args && args.briefs) || DEFAULT_BRIEFS
const DRAFT_SCHEMA = { type: 'object', properties: { slug: { type: 'string' }, parent: { type: 'string' }, title: { type: 'string' }, testable_claim: { type: 'string' }, body_path: { type: 'string' }, files: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } } }, required: ['slug', 'parent', 'title', 'testable_claim', 'body_path'] }
const CRITIC_SCHEMA = { type: 'object', properties: { ok: { type: 'boolean' }, fixed: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } }, left: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } }, word_counts: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } } }, required: ['ok', 'fixed', 'left'] }
phase('Draft')
const drafts = (await parallel(BRIEFS.map(b => () => agent(`${RULES}\n\nYOUR BRIEF: slug "${b.slug}", parent ${b.parent}. Scope: ${b.scope}\nTitle: a short imperative noun phrase. Write the body to ${SCRATCH}/${b.slug}.md.`, { label: `draft:${b.slug}`, phase: 'Draft', schema: DRAFT_SCHEMA, model: MODEL, effort: EFFORT })))).filter(Boolean)
log(`${drafts.length}/${BRIEFS.length} briefs drafted`)
phase('Critic')
const critic = await agent(`${RULES}\n\nYOU ARE THE CRITIC. ${drafts.length} brief bodies are in ${SCRATCH}/*.md (drafted by ${drafts.length} agents: ${drafts.map(d => d.slug).join(', ')}). Check each against the owner section and fix IN PLACE (you may edit files under ${SCRATCH} only): (a) any statement presented as an owner decision that is not in the owner verbatim text gets relabelled "(director proposal)" or removed; (b) the model table is identical wherever it appears and follows owner correction (4): prime Fable 5.1 max ultracode; quorum advisors Opus 5 MAX; director-kids Opus 5 HIGH; liaison Sonnet 5 high; pi parents/kids unchanged — and the comms rule of (4) is stated wherever comms are designed: collapsed ladder = director-kids have NO free comms to Belam; expanded hierarchy = free director-kid lateral comms and limited director-kid vertical comms to other director-kids; no director reaches Belam except through the quorum; the quorum IS Belam to anyone else; (c) no "&&" anywhere; (d) every FILES path exists (ls) unless marked NEW; (e) body ≤ 450 words, no frontmatter, no title line; (f) the briefs do not contradict each other on where the seat registry lives or how a seat is addressed (seat name); (g) NOT IN SCOPE lines point at the right sibling slug. Also write ${SCRATCH}/index.json = the array of {slug,parent,title,testable_claim,body_path} you were given, corrected if a title/claim needed it. Return {ok, fixed, left, word_counts}. Given drafts: ${JSON.stringify(drafts)}`, { label: 'critic', phase: 'Critic', schema: CRITIC_SCHEMA, model: MODEL, effort: EFFORT })
return { drafts, critic }